d a medal in 1997 in St. Moritz. The reigning world champions were the Swiss team of " [:a {:href "/athletes/86350"} "Reto Götschi"] "  and " [:a {:href "/athletes/83159"} "Guido Acklin"] "  but there was no shortage of possible title challengers. Canadian " [:a {:href "/athletes/91934"} "Pierre Leuders"] "  and " [:a {:href "/athletes/87207"} "Günther Huber"] "  of Italy had impressed over the World Cup season and both American and Latvian crews had high hopes."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The pattern for the competition was set in the first run with Italy and Canada jostling for the gold medal position. This continued for the next two runs and at the start of the final run Canada were just three hundredths of a second behind their Italian rivals. Pierre Leuders’ final run was slightly faster than Günther Huber’s and when the clock stopped at the finish line he had made up exactly that deficit and no more. This was not the first time that two crews had dead-heated for first place in Olympic competition but the tie-breaker rule which had decided the winners of the 1968 two man event had been abandoned by 1998 and the two crews shared the gold medal."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Pair"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Time"] [:th {} "Run #1"] [:th {} "Run #2"] [:th {} "Run #3"] [:th {} "Run #4"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "=1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/87207"} "Günther Huber"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/98170"} "Antonio Tartaglia"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ITA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/ITA.png"}] "ITA"]] [:td {} "3:37.24"] [:td {} "54.51 (1)"] [:td {} "54.29 (2)"] [:td {} "54.17 (3)"] [:td {} "54.27 (2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "=1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/91934"} "Pierre Lueders"] " / " [:a {:h